WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:01.513
You don't need to learn to code is

2
00:00:01.633 --> 00:00:03.254
true now in a way it wasn't in

3
00:00:03.274 --> 00:00:03.895
2022.

4
00:00:05.176 --> 00:00:07.078
But that doesn't mean code no longer

5
00:00:07.138 --> 00:00:07.519
matters.

6
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.203
The code still exists, it still runs, and

7
00:00:11.223 --> 00:00:12.304
it can still be wrong.

8
00:00:12.844 --> 00:00:15.427
What really changed is who writes it, not

9
00:00:15.487 --> 00:00:16.388
whether it gets written.

10
00:00:17.189 --> 00:00:18.871
Everything we talk about in this course

11
00:00:19.011 --> 00:00:20.432
hinges on that distinction.

12
00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:23.095
If you skip this point, the first time

13
00:00:23.175 --> 00:00:25.437
a tool fails, you'll think the tool is

14
00:00:25.498 --> 00:00:27.299
broken.

15
00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:29.243
Let's break down what we mean by building

16
00:00:29.304 --> 00:00:29.825
software.

17
00:00:30.346 --> 00:00:32.230
There are three main jobs involved.

18
00:00:32.791 --> 00:00:34.736
First, there's deciding what to build.

19
00:00:35.498 --> 00:00:37.201
This means figuring out what problem

20
00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:39.567
you're solving, for whom, and what it must

21
00:00:39.627 --> 00:00:39.807
do.

22
00:00:40.389 --> 00:00:42.553
Second is writing the code, which is

23
00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.778
turning those decisions into instructions

24
00:00:44.999 --> 00:00:47.223
a computer can follow.

25
00:00:47.223 --> 00:00:49.727
And finally, keeping it running, which

26
00:00:49.827 --> 00:00:52.752
involves hosting, managing data, accounts,

27
00:00:52.892 --> 00:00:54.535
and fixing issues when they arise.

28
00:00:55.116 --> 00:00:57.319
People often mix these three jobs under

29
00:00:57.359 --> 00:00:59.623
the term coding, which is why learn to

30
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:01.726
code sounded like the only door in.

31
00:01:02.508 --> 00:01:04.571
Let's look at that in a picture.

32
00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:07.554
Now, out of those three jobs, only the

33
00:01:07.594 --> 00:01:08.956
middle one got automated.

34
00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:12.300
Generative AI is really good at producing

35
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.902
code from a clear description.

36
00:01:14.543 --> 00:01:16.345
But it struggles with knowing which thing

37
00:01:16.405 --> 00:01:17.186
is worth building.

38
00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:19.729
It's also getting better at the third job,

39
00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:21.412
but it's not quite there yet.

40
00:01:22.092 --> 00:01:24.636
So, the skill that matters now is being

41
00:01:24.756 --> 00:01:26.979
able to describe precisely what you want.

42
00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:30.082
And that's a skill you already have, from

43
00:01:30.243 --> 00:01:31.744
every time you've briefed someone on

44
00:01:31.805 --> 00:01:32.986
something.

45
00:01:33.386 --> 00:01:35.368
You might be surprised to learn that most

46
00:01:35.408 --> 00:01:37.310
of the people using these tools aren't

47
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:37.910
developers.

48
00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.813
They're founders, product managers,

49
00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:42.615
designers, and marketers.

50
00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.138
They're not building the next operating

51
00:01:45.218 --> 00:01:45.618
system.

52
00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:48.341
Instead, they're creating internal tools,

53
00:01:48.801 --> 00:01:51.063
landing pages, booking forms, and

54
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:52.765
prototypes that help get meetings.

55
00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:54.987
This isn't a lesser category.

56
00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.230
In fact, most software that earns its keep

57
00:01:58.351 --> 00:01:59.832
falls into this space.

58
00:02:00.521 --> 00:02:02.385
By the end of this course, you'll have

59
00:02:02.425 --> 00:02:04.610
built a real application with a real

60
00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.737
database, one that other people can access

61
00:02:07.837 --> 00:02:09.802
on the internet at an address you own.

62
00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:12.107
You'll also develop the habit of

63
00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:14.312
describing what you want clearly enough to

64
00:02:14.352 --> 00:02:14.612
get it.

65
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:16.557
And you'll gain a clear sense of the

66
00:02:16.737 --> 00:02:18.661
edge — that point where you should stop

67
00:02:18.761 --> 00:02:19.964
and hire someone.

68
00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:23.042
Now, let's talk about some honest caveats.

69
00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:26.328
These tools do cost money, and the cost

70
00:02:26.408 --> 00:02:27.470
grows with your usage.

71
00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:30.235
They produce code that you might not read,

72
00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:32.880
and sometimes that code can be wrong in

73
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.603
ways that only show up later.

74
00:02:35.184 --> 00:02:37.068
If you're dealing with payments, health

75
00:02:37.108 --> 00:02:40.334
data, or anyone's private information, you

76
00:02:40.394 --> 00:02:42.337
definitely want a professional's eyes on

77
00:02:42.418 --> 00:02:44.681
it before it goes live.

78
00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:46.924
Lesson twelve will revisit all of this

79
00:02:47.004 --> 00:02:47.465
properly.

80
00:02:48.005 --> 00:02:49.727
It’s the most important lesson in this

81
00:02:49.788 --> 00:02:52.151
course, and it's intentionally saved for

82
00:02:52.191 --> 00:02:52.571
last.

83
00:02:53.753 --> 00:02:54.534
So what's next?

84
00:02:55.214 --> 00:02:57.077
In the next lesson, we'll map out what

85
00:02:57.157 --> 00:02:59.180
each tool in this world is actually for.

86
00:02:59.961 --> 00:03:02.764
This way, the names stop being noise, and

87
00:03:02.804 --> 00:03:04.326
you can see how they fit into what

88
00:03:04.346 --> 00:03:05.808
you're trying to accomplish.

